    "body": "## Problem\n\n`vim.fn.wait()` can overrun its timeout when every condition evaluation closes a libuv handle or schedules a callback. The shared event-loop macro discards each pass's fractional milliseconds, so fast passes can deduct no time at all.\n\n## Solution\n\nAdvance the time baseline only by the whole milliseconds deducted from the remaining timeout. This carries the fraction into the next pass without constructing a deadline. The initial condition check and zero/negative timeout behavior stay unchanged.\n\nAdd bounded timer-close and scheduled-callback regressions. They check the timeout result and elapsed duration, so rounding every pass up to a millisecond cannot make them pass by returning early.\n\nValidated on macOS arm64: `wait_spec` and the related job, channel, remote, server, exit, Lua-eval and API specs passed (4 skips across those specs). Lua/C lint and formatting checks passed. `make unittest` is blocked by a LuaJIT/macOS SDK FFI error that also occurs on the unchanged base.\n\nLua's `vim.wait()` already tracks fractional time; this patch addresses the shared C path used by Vimscript.\n\nAI-assisted\n",
